    <p>Azoth is an open-source AI model for malware detection. It
    classifies samples from <a href="/cleave/">cleave</a>'s capability
    output, covering ELF, PE, Mach-O, Java .class, Python .pyc, and
    compiled AppleScript, plus source-code ecosystems like Python, npm,
    and VS Code extensions.</p>

    <p>It runs locally on the host calling it. No network round-trips, no
    telemetry. <a href="/litmus/">litmus</a> is the reference scanner,
    but azoth isn't bound to it — the weights, the training pipeline, and
    the capability schema are Apache 2.0, so any tool can drive the model
    or retrain it on its own data.</p>

    <h2>Why a small, dedicated model</h2>

    <p><strong>Cost and throughput.</strong> Per-token pricing doesn't
    pencil out for continuous scanning. Every dependency update, every
    CI run, every artifact in a registry is another classification —
    multiplied across a fleet, the metered models become the dominant
    line item before they become the dominant signal. Azoth runs on CPU
    at zero marginal cost per call, so the limit is wall clock, not the
    invoice.</p>

    <p><strong>Footprint.</strong> No GPU, no accelerator. The training
    pipeline distills from analyst-labeled malware corpora, public
    threat reports, and frontier LLMs into a model that fits on a
    Raspberry Pi. The same weights run on a laptop, a CI runner, or an
    endpoint agent.</p>

    <p><strong>Format-agnostic input.</strong> Azoth classifies on
    cleave's structured capabilities, not raw bytes or disassembly
    tokens. That puts the abstraction above format and language: one
    model handles every format cleave decomposes, and surface-level
    changes — repacking, string mangling,
    recompilation — don't move the features the model sees. Capability
    extraction has its own evasion surface (cleave's coverage of
    packers, dynamic loaders, and obfuscated control flow), and we
    document it rather than hide it.</p>

    <p><strong>License.</strong> Apache 2.0 on the weights, the training
    pipeline, and the runtime. Embed it in a scanner, a CI gate, or an
    EDR pipeline without a commercial conversation.</p>

    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p>In active development. Initial weights, evaluation results, and
    the training pipeline will land on
    <a href="https://codeberg.org/atomdrift" target="_blank">Codeberg</a>
    with the first release. Until then, treat anything on this page as
    the design intent — not a benchmark.</p>

